H₃ and H₂O in increasing boiling point.","explanation":"CH₄ has dispersion forces only; NH₃ hydrogen-bonds, while water forms a stronger, more extensive network.","questionKind":"application","difficulty":"hard","cognitive":"analysis","topic":"Intermolecular Forces and Physical Properties","fingerprint":"90920421895ea5474c37310fa3a85b031e8825db7ae1fb57ccabeb0b80d5c055","studyRef":{"sourceLabel":"Period 6; CHM 101 Chemical Bonding slides; Chapter 7","sourceKind":"supplied_material","reviewedFromLocalFiles":true},"generationMeta":{"import":"codex_chm101_240_v1","sourceReference":"Period 6; CHM 101 Chemical Bonding slides; Chapter 7","calculationAndConceptCheckCompleted":true,"humanVerificationRequired":true},"options":[{"id":"92b97c49-34c8-5107-b220-b0cef114a4f0","text":"H₂O < NH₃ < CH₄","isCorrect":false,"position":0},{"id":"0c08b5fe-2eb3-5218-9f6e-8f930711de62","text":"NH₃ < H₂O < CH₄","isCorrect":false,"position":1},{"id":"0c57e958-a503-5d45-82e7-e6bfd84e5df4","text":"CH₄ < H₂O < NH₃","isCorrect":false,"position":2},{"id":"5f6b36f5-d087-544d-861b-02213acc21e0","text":"CH₄ < NH₃ < H₂O","isCorrect":true,"position":3}]},{"id":"8a5de31a-35cb-58b9-9c20-abae6a7072e9","position":134,"prompt":"Which should have the higher boiling point, pentane or neopentane, and why?","explanation":"The less compact pentane molecule has greater surface contact and stronger dispersion attractions.","questionKind":"application","difficulty":"hard","cognitive":"analysis","topic":"Intermolecular Forces and Physical Properties","fingerprint":"3cbde0ed65be057aeff30ed90ad849c5b6dc46c891c077c4d86540ad499023df","studyRef":{"sourceLabel":"Period 6; CHM 101 Chemical Bonding slides; Chapter 7","sourceKind":"supplied_material","reviewedFromLocalFiles":true},"generationMeta":{"import":"codex_chm101_240_v1","sourceReference":"Period 6; CHM 101 Chemical Bonding slides; Chapter 7","calculationAndConceptCheckCompleted":true,"humanVerificationRequired":true},"options":[{"id":"0cefd212-c471-51f8-97df-38666dc